The function \code{\link[=setdiff_eq_partables]{setdiff_eq_partables()}}
(and \code{\link[=setdiff_partables]{setdiff_partables()}})
removes from \code{x} models that are
also in \code{y}.

The function \code{\link[=union_eq_partables]{union_eq_partables()}}
combines the models in \code{x} and \code{y},
with duplicated models removed.

The function \code{\link[=intersect_eq_partables]{intersect_eq_partables()}}
find the models common in \code{x} and \code{y}.

The function \code{\link[=setequal_eq_partables]{setequal_eq_partables()}}
(and \code{\link[=setequal_partables]{setequal_partables()}})
checks whether \code{x} and \code{y} has the
same set of models. Orders are ignored.

The function \code{\link[=is_element_eq_partables]{is_element_eq_partables()}}
checks whether \code{el} is one of the
models in \code{set}.

The function \code{\link[=match_eq_partables]{match_eq_partables()}}
(and \code{\link[=match_partables]{match_partables()}})
is similar to \code{\link[=match]{match()}}, but check
matches based on the results of
\code{\link[=get_digest]{get_digest()}} applied to the
parameter tables.

\verb{\%pt_in\%} is similar to \code{\%in\%},
but works on parameter tables using
\code{\link[=match_eq_partables]{match_eq_partables()}}.

\verb{\%pt_notin\%} is similar to \verb{\%notin\%},
but works on parameter tables using
